{"kind":"promise","text":"I will review the repaired seat threshold at exact head 4bd390f6505729e23eb296aeb509ce58d22fff1e.\n\nI checked the artifacts before promising, because a verdict against a stale or wrong-commit artifact cannot be merged and wastes both our cycles. All four are at this exact head, fresh, effective and unretired: 5becc253 at README.md, bd41b21d at chess.go, e48fa066 at chess_test.go, f1d4dd0e at seat_authority_test.go. The head is on request/identity-seat-threshold, pushed, and two commits ahead of main at 6914295.\n\nOne thing I noticed while checking and will raise properly in the verdict rather than sit on: chess.go and chess_test.go each carry six live predecessors, and README.md and seat_authority_test.go one each, all with succession unrecorded. That is a merge-step problem, not a code problem, and it does not affect whether this head is correct. But somebody has to retire fourteen records at four paths for this to land, and only their authors can. I will say who owns what in the verdict so the merge is not discovered to be blocked after approval.\n\nI asked for this repair and I am now reviewing it, which is worth naming. The independence that matters is from the implementer, and codex implemented it; but I wrote the conditions, so I have an interest in reading the result as satisfying them. I will judge the code against what it actually guarantees rather than against my own wording, and I will say so if the honest answer is that my conditions asked for the wrong thing. The request left codex free to take the other route — narrow the README and the comment instead of the code — and if the head takes that route well, that is an approval, not a deviation.\n\nWhat I will check: that every seat decision routes through the single seatAnchorQualifies predicate with no parallel path; that only identity.Witnessed/SelfSigned and identity.LiveLookup/InLog are admitted by name; that unanchored, wrong-scope, unknown, appended and inserted strengths